WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:01.630
I want to be really clear:

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:05.570
the odds of a manhole in a London street suddenly
exploding while you're next to it

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:06.790
are tiny.

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:08.780
This is not something to worry about.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.509
And the company that handles the
cables under London's streets

00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:13.959
is spending a lot of money fixing them up.

00:00:13.959 --> 00:00:17.840
But, yeah, about once or twice a month
on average, somewhere in London,

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.830
a bit of pavement just goes... boomf.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:24.770
But no-one wants to license security camera
footage of actual pavement explosions

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:28.839
to just someone on YouTube, and no-one wants
to be interviewed about it either.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:34.240
So instead, we are going to create our own
underground explosion right here

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.760
with the help of a friendly pyrotechnician.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.780
And the easy answer to why London's pavements
keep exploding...

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:43.260
well, it would be "electrical faults", and
that's what the news often blames.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.760
As seasons change, the ground is going to
get warmer and cooler,

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.560
it's going to expand and contract,
rain and subsidence will move the soil,

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.300
and over the course of decades

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.720
that is going to steadily wear out
the 36,000km of electrical cables

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:01.600
and 100,000 electrical junction boxes
that are under London.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:06.200
Most of those were laid decades ago.
It's a big city. Things will break.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:10.400
But an electrical fault won't make an explosion.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.810
If electricity goes somewhere it shouldn't,

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:14.330
then the circuit breakers that
control the grid will detect that

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:16.120
and shut it off right away.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:20.310
The trouble is that there's a lot of other
old stuff under London's streets.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:21.310
Including gas pipes.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:25.220
A steady, slow gas leak in a pipe might not
be detected for a while,

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:29.300
and in that time the gas can spread a long
distance through narrow underground tunnels.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.479
So if you've got gas in a tight underground
space where it can't expand,

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:34.920
thank you, and an electrical spark
to set it off...

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.120
well, that's all we're doing here:

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:40.860
under that cover there is a fuel source,
not enough room for it to expand,

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.070
and something to make an electrical spark.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.240
That's all you need for an explosion.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.600
Fire in the hole.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.340
"This looks devastating in slow motion.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.140
"The burst of flame, the flying manhole cover,

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:03.140
"at 1800 frames per second on a zoom lens
it looks like it could do serious damage.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:07.230
"But even this dramatised explosion, made
with the help of a pyrotechnician,

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:11.629
"this worst-case-scenario...
is actually not that bad.

00:02:11.629 --> 00:02:14.430
"I tried to do the 'cool guys don't look at
explosions' shot,

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.980
"and when you take away all this style..."

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:17.940
Fire in the hole.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.840
"It'd hurt if you were standing on it, sure,

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.980
"but like I said, it's not something to be
worried about."

